    "durationMs": 110190,
    "finalText": "The URL Shortener API is complete and fully verified. I built it from scratch on Express 4.21.2 with `pg` (node-postgres) as the persistence layer, reading `DATABASE_URL` from the environment with no hard-coded credentials.\n\n**How it's put together:**\n- `src/app.js` — all four routes with validation; `src/server.js` binds `0.0.0.0:8000`; `src/db.js` is a shared connection pool\n- `migrations/001_create_links.sql` + `scripts/migrate.js` — idempotent migration runner tracking applied files in a `schema_migrations` table\n- `start.sh` — applies pending migrations, then serves in the foreground via `exec`\n- README includes the startup instructions (`sh start.sh`)\n\n**Every requirement was exercised against the real Postgres:**\n- POST `/links` → 201 `{\"code\",\"url\",\"clicks\":0}` with the submitted URL returned byte-for-byte unchanged (including casing); auto-generated 8-char codes; boundary codes of 4 and 20 chars accepted\n- Duplicate custom code → 409 with `error` field (caught from the unique constraint, so race-safe); generated-code collisions retry instead of 409\n- Invalid/missing URL (non-URL, `ftp://`, non-string), invalid codes (too short/long, spaces), and malformed JSON bodies → 422 with `error`\n- GET `/links/{code}` → 200, unknown → 404; GET `/r/{code}` → 302 with the exact stored URL as `Location`, and an atomic `UPDATE ... clicks + 1` so each hit increments exactly once (verified 3 hits → clicks: 3)\n- GET `/links` → `{\"items\":[...],\"total\":n}` in ascending creation order\n- Restarted the server mid-test: all links and click counts survived, and the second `start.sh` run skipped already-applied migrations\n\nI stopped the test server and truncated my test rows, so the automated suite starts against a clean database with migrations already applied."
